# REFUTATION — cycle 67, M3a "the four SR rows and nothing else"

## 0. The R4 failure first: same property, two spellings — and the gate was wrong, not the peer

**`Coerce Dot?` and `Coerced?` are the SAME property.** LabVIEW resolves the **ID**, not the string. The LabVIEW Wiki records `634A006` on the Terminal class as Short Name `Coerced?`, Long Name `Coercion Dot?`, Boolean, read-only, introduced LabVIEW 2018. The machine built a property node from that same ID on LabVIEW 2026 and its one non-standard terminal reads `Coerce Dot?` (`diag_c66c_coercion.log:44`). An ID cannot resolve to two different properties; what differs is the **display string NI ships for the property-node item**, re-spelled between the version the wiki's community dump captured and 26.3.1f1. The control case proves the route is sound: `634A008` resolved and its terminal read exactly `Data Type` (`:54-56`).

**So LabVIEW 2026's name for a terminal's coercion-dot flag is `Coerce Dot?`**, and `docs/cycle27-plan.md:2690` already says so.

**R4 gates on a third-party documentation string, not on the machine.** R3 — "exactly ONE non-standard terminal" — is the real assertion and it passed (`:45`). R4 should record the name as a FACT. As written it will fail again on any property NI has re-spelled, and it turned a 44-pass run into `rc=1`.

**One correction to your framing.** You wrote that the coercion question "stayed UNMEASURED" *because* the name was wrong. The log says otherwise: coercion is unmeasured because **no wrapped column of the op fleet carries a coercion flag or a data type, and reading a VALUE needs a new saved op VI** (`:26-28`, `:186-188`). R4 blocks nothing. Conflating the two makes a name-string gate look like a measurement blocker, and would justify building a value-reading op to answer a question the same log calls irrelevant to a pure scheduling move.

