The 12 severances decompose exactly: 7 internal to the moved set (restored by M2) + 5 external. Four are your rows. **The fifth is `#10407` t1 `# slices in stack`.**

This is not a new discovery — it is **your own known-hard row**:

- `docs/d1-build-plan.md:429` — *"t1 `# slices in stack` ← **LoopTunnel #9641, IndexMode 0**, loop-invariant."* Its designed carrier is a **tunnel**. No shift register can carry it.
- `docs/d1-route-b-plan.md:271` — *"`#10407` t1 `# slices in stack` ← LoopTunnel `#9641` is 🔴 **R1**."*
- `tools/bench/task_run8_predictions.md:10` — *"The single NO-ROUTE (`:430`) is `#10407 t1 '# slices in stack' <- from-tunnel 9641, w9649 has 0 source terminals, not 1: []`."* The one row route B never wired.
- `docs/frame-loop-wire-graph.md:465` lists **9635 among the frame-loop LoopTunnel inner-side wires** — so it runs from `#9641`'s inner side on `#637` to `#10407` t1. Moving `#10407` into `#23032` makes it cross a **new** border, and `LoopTunnel` held at **135** at all three census points (`:30`, `:284`, `:441`): **no tunnel was ever created on `#23032`.**

The tunnel did not vanish with its wire — `Tunnel` held at 471 throughout. A case-structure input tunnel with no external source is an unwired/undirected tunnel, which LabVIEW does not tolerate.

Even granting uncertainty about whether that one bare tunnel *alone* forces `ExecState` 0, your words are **"and nothing else"**. A fifth severed row exists, so the claim is false as stated either way. Note that `docs/cycle27-plan.md:2698` already hedges this — **the claim is stronger than the plan it cites.**

